WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.329 --> 00:00:05.147
Did you guys hear that?

00:00:05.147 --> 00:00:09.376
Imagine it: You’re home alone. Something goes bump in the night. 

00:00:09.376 --> 00:00:13.596
What was it? The cat? A burglar? Or… maybe a ghost?

00:00:13.596 --> 00:00:16.864
Our minds are big and they require furnishing. 

00:00:16.864 --> 00:00:22.838
For humans, any answer is preferable to the terror
of not knowing, so we automatically create causes

00:00:22.838 --> 00:00:26.708
to go with our effects — conventional or otherwise.

00:00:26.708 --> 00:00:31.194
Unfortunately, the real world is complicated, and cause and effect are murky.

00:00:31.194 --> 00:00:36.081
Even in carefully controlled studies, what works in one place
might not work somewhere else.

00:00:36.081 --> 00:00:40.631
And if an experiment can’t be replicated, should it be trusted in the first place?

00:00:40.631 --> 00:00:47.067
As historian Daniel Boorstin said, “The greatest obstacle to [progress]
is not ignorance, but the illusion of knowledge.”

00:00:47.067 --> 00:00:53.050
Harvard professor Vikram Mansharamani recommends diversifying
our sources and developing our own judgement,

00:00:53.050 --> 00:00:59.938
rather than relying just on experts. Our decisions are always
based on partial knowledge, and that can be scary. 

00:00:59.938 --> 00:01:06.227
But in a world  defined by contingency and uncertainty,
we just have to take a chance.

00:01:07.229 --> 00:01:12.189
When it comes to public policy, what are you most afraid of?
Let us know in our poll.

00:01:12.189 --> 00:01:17.154
Also, let us know what other topics you'd like
